Apple's Passwords app on Mac will soon let you choose when it automatically locks. Spotted by code explorer <a href="https://x.com/itspdfu/status/2119153160954564801">pdfu</a>, a new menu in macOS 27.3 beta offers one, three, or 10 minutes of inactivity, alongside options to lock when the Mac itself locks or sleeps, or for it to never automatically lock.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/passwords-app-macos-27-3@2x-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="2560" height="1440" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1076464" /><br/>
In the current macOS 27.2 general release and in earlier versions of Apple's Mac operating system, the Passwords app uses a fixed three-minute inactivity timeout. So when the update arrives in December, you'll have more control over how long the app stays unlocked, rather than have to rely on the default timeout.<br/>
<br/>
